// Accidentals of the same note name an octave apart share ONE column, whatever the
// octave — E#4 with E#5, C#4 with C#5. AccidentalPlacement reads the note name off
// the staff position with a FLOORED modulo, ((p % 7) + 7) % 7; C#'s own `%` truncates
// towards zero, so positions on OPPOSITE SIDES of the middle line would come out as
// two different note names and each pair would be split into two columns.
// LILYPOND-REF: lily/accidental-placement.cc acc_less / set_ape_skylines.
//
// ⚠️ THE POSITIONS HAVE TO STRADDLE ZERO, and until 2026-08-16 they did not. The
// header named "staff positions 3 (E5) and -4 (E4)" while the music wrote
// <eis' eis'' cis' cis''> = positions 3 and 10, 1 and 8 — all non-negative, where
// truncating and floored agree. Measured: replacing the floored modulo with a bare
// `%` left this book's SVG BYTE-IDENTICAL. It is now 3/-4 (E) and 1/-6 (C), and the
// same poison splits it into three columns and pushes one accidental out to x 10.14.
//
// Verified on the twin against LilyPond 2.26.0: LP puts the four sharps in TWO
// columns, x 7.885 and 9.169, and Lily# draws them at 7.89 and 9.17 (its SVG rounds
// to two places). ⚠️ LP gives those same two columns for the OLD spelling as well —
// it works from pitches and cannot see this defect at all, so LP agreement is not
// what makes a book here useful. Straddling zero is.
title "Accidental octave straddle"
octave absolute
part m { clef treble }
section Main {
  m { <eis eis' cis cis'>1 | }
}
form main { Main }
score main "accidental-octave-straddle" { staff m }
